/* ============================================================
   Maths past-paper questions

   299 questions from 22 whole papers, carrying 2143 marks. Complete
   papers, so a practice test can be built out of the real thing
   rather than a selection from it.

   The tariff and the page range are the paper's own: every question
   closes with "(Total for Question 7 is 9 marks)", which is what the
   extraction is anchored on. Detecting where a question STARTS is
   fragile - a stray margin fragment sits above the number often
   enough to weld two questions together - and it is what made every
   Paper 3 come to half its marks, because that paper restarts its
   numbering at Mechanics and the second half read as duplicates.

   THE CHAPTER IS INFERRED AND NEVER CREDITS A RATING.

   A past paper is not filed by topic, so the chapter has to be
   guessed from the wording. It is guessed reasonably well: the
   topic compilations ARE filed by chapter, so their questions are
   labelled data, and each chapter is learned as a centroid of its
   own real exam wording with terms weighted by rarity. That beat
   hand-written keyword lists outright - those had Circles winning
   51 questions, because a third of its list was words every Pure
   question uses.

   But measured honestly, on held-out questions, it is 67% right
   overall, and 74% right on the ones it feels confident about.
   Reaching 90% means keeping only 12% of them. That is fine for
   browsing and filtering and nowhere near good enough to move a RAG
   rating, so these never credit a chapter when a test is marked -
   `guess` is true on all of them by design, not by accident.
   `likely` marks the ones above the 87%-precision threshold, and is
   only used to word the label.

   Past-paper topic attribution that IS trusted lives in the Past
   Papers section, where you assign the topic of each lost mark
   yourself. A person reading their own script beats this.
   ============================================================ */

